{"content":[{"c":["RAHAB* (Monster)"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Mythological sea monster that poetically represented Egypt (",{"href":"?bref=Ps.87.4","r":"Ps.87.4","rawR":"Ps.87.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.87.4","value":"Ps.87.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 87:4"},"). “For Egypt’s help is worthless and empty, therefore I have called her ‘Rahab who sits still’ ” (",{"href":"?bref=Isa.30.7","r":"Isa.30.7","rawR":"Isa.30.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.30.7","value":"Isa.30.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 30:7"},", ",{"c":["rsv"],"sourceClass":"sc","t":"span"},"). The biblical writers, with Israel’s crossing of the Red Sea and the Egyptian army’s subsequent drowning (cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.51.10","r":"Isa.51.10","rawR":"Isa.51.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.51.10","value":"Isa.51.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 51:10"},"), portray God as waging war against this monster and cruelly defeating it (",{"href":"?bref=Job.26.12","r":"Job.26.12","rawR":"Job.26.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Job.26.12","value":"Job.26.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Job 26:12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.89.10","r":"Ps.89.10","rawR":"Ps.89.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.89.10","value":"Ps.89.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 89:10"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Isa.51.9","r":"Isa.51.9","rawR":"Isa.51.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Isa.51.9","value":"Isa.51.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"Is 51:9"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=EgyptEgyptian_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"EgyptEgyptian_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"EgyptEgyptian_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Egypt, Egyptian"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"RahabMonster","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Ps 87:4","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Ps.87.4","rawQueryValue":"Ps.87.4","source